         text="Once a year the assembly could vote to exile one man for ten years without trial. Citizens scratched a name on a potsherd in a fenced area of the Agora; with 6,000 votes cast, the man named most often had to leave. The victor of Salamis was sent away about 471. A well on the north slope held 190 sherds with his name, written by a few hands: ballots prepared for distribution.",
