         text="Athens had sided with Mithridates against Rome. After a winter siege, with the people eating boiled leather, Sulla's legions broke through the wall between the Piraeus and Sacred Gates at midnight. Plutarch says the blood in the Agora flooded the Kerameikos and ran out through the Dipylon. Sulla spared the city 'for the sake of the dead' but cut down the groves of the Academy and the Lyceum.",
